1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,540
Dodgeball.

2
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:01,640
Last one standing.

3
00:00:02,220 --> 00:00:04,960
Your friend winds up and throws, hard, straight

4
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:05,600
at your face.

5
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:06,440
You flinch.

6
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:08,840
You squeeze your eyes shut and think one

7
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:09,360
word.

8
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:10,520
Stop.

9
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:12,400
And everything does.

10
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:14,910
The ball is hanging in the air, ten

11
00:00:14,910 --> 00:00:16,060
centimetres from your nose.

12
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:19,620
Your friend is frozen mid-laugh.

13
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:22,400
A kid in the stands is frozen mid-sneeze.

14
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,280
And it is completely, perfectly silent.

15
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:27,940
So what if you could stop time?

16
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:29,380
Not slow it down.

17
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:30,320
Stop it.

18
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:32,040
For everyone except you.

19
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:35,000
The first thing you notice is the quiet.

20
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:36,660
No squeaking shoes.

21
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:37,860
No shouting.

22
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:39,220
Not even a hum.

23
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:41,540
That's because sound is air moving.

24
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:44,220
When someone shouts, they push air molecules, which

25
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:45,960
push the next ones, all the way to

26
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:46,400
your ear.

27
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,960
Freeze the air, and there's nothing left to

28
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:49,560
carry a sound.

29
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:50,840
You clap your hands.

30
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:51,980
Nothing.

31
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:54,500
Then you try to walk, and it's like

32
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:55,800
walking into a mattress.

33
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:58,140
Air looks like nothing, but it's packed with

34
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:00,573
molecules, normally zooming around at about five hundred

35
00:01:00,573 --> 00:01:01,560
metres a second.

36
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:02,940
That's faster than a jet airliner.

37
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:04,740
They usually bounce out of your way.

38
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:07,360
Frozen, they don't move at all.

39
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:09,900
Physicists who've thought about this say pushing through

40
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,040
frozen air would take enormous force.

41
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:13,760
You wouldn't be walking through air.

42
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:15,880
You'd be shoving through a wall of it.

43
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:17,880
And it gets worse.

44
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:20,700
To see anything, light has to travel from

45
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:22,220
the world into your eyes.

46
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:25,340
A few tiny packets of light, called photons,

47
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:26,500
have to land on the back of your

48
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:26,780
eye.

49
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:29,760
If time stops, light stops too.

50
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:31,260
Nothing new reaches you.

51
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:34,000
One philosopher of physics put it simply: if

52
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,140
you really stopped time, you probably couldn't see

53
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:37,500
a thing.

54
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:40,860
Silent, solid, and pitch black.

55
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:42,100
Not a superpower.

56
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:43,280
A prison.

57
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:46,860
So, like every story that does this, let's

58
00:01:46,860 --> 00:01:47,020
cheat.

59
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:50,020
Time stops everywhere, except in a little bubble

60
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:50,540
around you.

61
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:53,120
Inside your bubble, the air moves, so you

62
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:54,660
can breathe, hear and walk.

63
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:56,880
Light inside it still works, so you can

64
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:57,080
see.

65
00:01:57,920 --> 00:01:59,700
Physicists will tell you there's no real science

66
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:00,420
that allows this.

67
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:02,700
But it's your power, so you get a

68
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:02,820
bubble.

69
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:04,980
You gently push the ball away from your

70
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:05,180
face.

71
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:06,500
You step out of the way.

72
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:07,700
Then you let go.

73
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:09,320
The ball sails past.

74
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:11,360
Your friend's mouth drops open.

75
00:02:11,820 --> 00:02:12,800
How did you dodge that?

76
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:14,020
Reflexes.

77
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:16,040
You start testing it.

78
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:17,300
And the bubble has rules.

79
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:20,040
It only reaches about an arm's length around

80
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:20,360
you.

81
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:23,420
Outside it, everything is frozen, including the air.

82
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:25,700
So there's no fresh air coming in.

83
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:28,040
Just whatever is in your bubble and in

84
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:28,360
your lungs.

85
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:30,520
Most people can hold their breath for somewhere

86
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:32,860
between thirty seconds and a minute and a

87
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:33,080
half.

88
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:36,300
Trained freedivers can go more than eleven minutes.

89
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:38,500
But your brain can start to be damaged

90
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:40,840
after about four minutes without oxygen.

91
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:43,340
So your superpower comes with a timer.

92
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:45,420
And you can't hear it ticking.

93
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:47,500
And here's the thing.

94
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:50,100
Scientists have actually stopped something.

95
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:50,860
Light.

96
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:53,820
In 2013, a team in Germany fired a

97
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:56,460
beam of light into a special crystal, then

98
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:57,720
switched off a second laser.

99
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:00,880
The light stopped inside the crystal.

100
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:02,180
For about a minute.

101
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:04,680
Then they switched the laser back on, and

102
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:06,400
the light came out again, carrying a tiny

103
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:07,760
picture of three stripes.

104
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:10,720
And in 2018, a camera at Caltech filmed

105
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:13,120
at ten trillion frames a second.

106
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:15,020
Fast enough to watch a pulse of light

107
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:15,400
move.

108
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:17,960
Your own brain even does a small version.

109
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:20,100
Look quickly at a clock with a second

110
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:22,060
hand, and the first second can seem to

111
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:23,460
last longer than the others.

112
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:25,960
It's called the stopped-clock illusion.

113
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:29,220
Then one Saturday, it matters.

114
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:31,460
Your friend's little brother chases a ball into

115
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:31,840
the road.

116
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:32,780
There's a van.

117
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:33,720
It's too close.

118
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:34,600
You stop time.

119
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:35,920
Everything freezes.

120
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:38,300
The van, the driver's face, the ball in

121
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:39,420
mid-bounce.

122
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:39,720
You run.

123
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:42,460
But your bubble only reaches arm's length, and

124
00:03:42,460 --> 00:03:44,400
every step you take, you're pushing your bubble

125
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:45,540
through frozen air.

126
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:46,560
Ten seconds.

127
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:47,200
Twenty.

128
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:48,140
Your chest is burning.

129
00:03:48,140 --> 00:03:49,000
Forty.

130
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:49,580
You reach him.

131
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:50,420
You grab his jacket.

132
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:51,740
You pull him back to the pavement.

133
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:52,920
Fifty seconds.

134
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:54,620
Your vision is going grey.

135
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:55,180
You let go.

136
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:56,380
The van roars past.

137
00:03:56,500 --> 00:03:57,760
The ball bounces off its bumper.

138
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:00,700
And he's standing next to you, totally confused.

139
00:04:01,460 --> 00:04:02,520
Where did you come from?

140
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:05,660
That night, you try it one more time.

141
00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:07,460
Just a few seconds, to check you're okay.

142
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:09,000
The street freezes.

143
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:10,780
Birds stuck in the sky.

144
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:12,980
A cyclist stuck mid-pedal.

145
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:15,820
And across the road, someone is still moving.

146
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:17,940
They look straight at you.

147
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:19,200
And they wave.

148
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:23,100
So if you could stop time, what would

149
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:24,560
you do with your sixty seconds?

150
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:25,720
Tell us in the comments.

151
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:28,460
And subscribe, because next time, we find out

152
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:30,280
what happens if you could turn invisible.
